[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Parallel Execution, Matrix Jobs, and Stage Concurrency in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern continuous integration pipelines demand speed and efficiency. Waiting for tests, builds, and deployments to run sequentially can severely bottleneck delivery cycles. Jenkins provides powerful mechanisms like parallel execution, matrix jobs, and stage concurrency to run multiple tasks simultaneously and optimize resource utilization."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Understanding Parallel Execution in Declarative Pipelines"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a Jenkins Declarative Pipeline, the parallel directive allows you to execute multiple steps or stages concurrently within a single stage. This is ideal for running independent test suites, such as unit tests, integration tests, and linters."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo 'Building application...'
            }
        }
        stage('Test Suite') {
            parallel {
                скопа('Unit Tests') {
                    steps {
                        echo 'Running unit tests...'
                    }
                }
                stage('Integration Tests') {
                    steps {
                        echo 'Running integration tests...'
                    }
                }
                stage('Linting') {
                    steps {
                        echo 'Running code linter...'
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. Leveraging Matrix Jobs for Multi-Configuration Builds"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you need to test your software across multiple environments, operating systems, or runtime versions, writing individual parallel blocks can become tedious. Jenkins Matrix Jobs allow you to define axes of configuration to automatically generate combinations of builds."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent none
    stages {
        stage('Matrix Build') {
            matrix {
                axes {
                    axis {
                        name 'PLATFORM'
                        values 'linux', 'windows', 'mac'
                    }
                    axis {
                        name 'NODE_VERSION'
                        values '16', '18', '20'
                    }
                }
                exclude {
                    // Exclude unstable or unsupported combinations
                    axis {
                        name 'PLATFORM'
                        values 'mac'
                    }
                    axis {
                        name 'NODE_VERSION'
                        values '16'
                    }
                }
                agent {
                    label "\${PLATFORM}"
                }
                stages {
                    stage('Build and Test') {
                        steps {
                            echo "Running on \${PLATFORM} with Node \${NODE_VERSION}"
                            sh 'node --version'
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"3. Managing Stage Concurrency and Resource Limits"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"While running everything in parallel is tempting, it can easily exhaust cluster resources, saturate database connections, or exceed API rate limits. Jenkins allows you to control concurrency using the options directive combined with throttleConcurrentBuilds or standard concurrency locks."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    options {
        // Limit total concurrent runs of this job globally or per node
        concurrency(maxConcurrent: 2)
    }
    stages {
        stage('Resource Heavy Stage') {
            steps {
                echo 'Executing tasks with restricted concurrency.'
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices for Parallel Pipelines"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Isolate Workspaces: Ensure parallel stages or matrix axes do not write to the same shared files without proper isolation to prevent race conditions."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Fail Fast: Use failFast true inside your parallel blocks if you want the entire stage to abort immediately when a single parallel branch fails."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Agent Provisioning: Ensure your Jenkins master has enough idle agents or dynamic cloud nodes (like Kubernetes pods) to handle the spike in concurrent job allocations."}]
